<p>Decimal 36869. Default value: 240. Also known as <strong>VICCR5</strong> and
<strong>SCREEN_CHAR_REGISTER</strong>.</p>
<p>Sets where the VIC chip reads the screen map and the character set from. The
register default of 240 (%11110000) means screen at $1E00 and characters from the
ROM character set at $8000.</p>

<p><strong>Bits 7-4</strong> serve as bits 13-10 of the screen map address, combined with
bit 7 of $9002 (SCREEN_TEXT_WIDTH) which supplies bit 9, to form the 14 bit VIC
chip address:</p>
<code>
                 76 54
14 bit address = 01 1110 0000 0000 = $1E00 (7680)
                      7
</code>
<p>Note the odd behaviour here: bit 7 must be written as 1, but the chip sees it as
a 0 when forming the address.</p>

<p><strong>Bits 3-0</strong> serve as bits 13-10 of the character set address. The default of
0 selects the ROM character set at $8000 (32768):</p>
<code>
                 32 10
14 bit address = 00 0000 0000 0000 = $8000 (32768)
</code>
<p>Bit 3 is essentially a ROM/RAM switch: when it is 1 the character set is read
from RAM ($0000-$1C00), when it is 0 it comes from ROM ($8000-$9C00). See VICCR5
for the full table of values.</p>

<p>Example</p>
<code>
// screen at $1E00, custom character set at $1C00 in RAM
VIC_DATA_LOC := %11111111;
</code>

<p><strong>On the C64, MEGA65 and C128</strong> the same constant name points at $D018
instead, which does the same job for the VIC-II: bits 7-4 select the screen
memory and bits 3-1 the character set, both relative to the current 16K VIC
bank.</p>
